   704	\section{Triangulations}
   705	
   706	We turn now to filling simplicial triangulations of $S^2$.
   707	Let's begin by establishing terminology.
   708	
   709	An \emph{$n$-simplex} $s$ is simply a set of size $n+1$.
   710	Its \emph{faces} are its subsets,
   711	which are $k$-simplices with $-1 \leq k \leq n$,
   712	$-1$ being the dimension of the empty simplex.
   713	A \emph{simplicial complex} $\sigma$ is a finite collection of simplices
   714	closed under taking faces:
   715	If $t \subset s \in \sigma$ then $t \in \sigma$.
   716	
   717	For any $k$-simplex $s \in \sigma$ define the \emph{link}
   718	\[
   719	\link(s,\sigma) = \{t: t \cap s = \emptyset, s \cup t \in \sigma \}
   720	.
   721	\]
   722	This is a simplicial complex, but
   723	(except for $\link(\emptyset,\sigma)=\sigma$)
   724	it is not a subcomplex of $\sigma$,
   725	because we are taking simplices in $\sigma$ and knocking down
   726	their dimension by $k+1$.
   727	
   728	We are interested in particularly nice simplicial complexes
   729	called normal pseudomanifolds, or as we will prefer to say,
   730	\emph{clean $n$-complexes}.
   731	
   732	\begin{enumerate}
   733	\item
   734	To start with,
   735	a clean complex must be \emph{pure},
   736	meaning that every simplex of $\sigma$ belongs to some $n$-simplex in $\sigma$.
   737	To determine $\sigma$ we can specify its $n$-simplices,
   738	and then throw in all their subsets.
   739	So we can confound a pure complex with the set of its $n$-simplices.
   740	
   741	\item
   742	A clean complex is a \emph{pseudomanifold}:
   743	Every $n$-simplex abuts at most one other $n$-simplex
   744	across any given $n-1$-simplex.
   745	Another way to say this is that
   746	the link $\link(s,\sigma)$ of any $n-1$-simplex $s$
   747	consists of either a single point 
   748	(in which case $s \in \del \sigma$ is a \emph{boundary simplex}),
   749	or two points
   750	($s \in \sigma \setminus \del \sigma$ is an \emph{interior simplex}.)
   751	
   752	\item
   753	A clean complex is \emph{normal}:
   754	For any simplex $s$ of dimension $0,\ldots,n-2$,
   755	$\link(s,\sigma)$ is connected.
   756	(Some definitions extend this requirement
   757	to the empty simplex of dimension $-1$;
   758	this forces $\sigma$ to be connected.)
   759	Being normal means that $\sigma$ is just what you get by gluing its 
   760	$n$-simplices along shared faces,
   761	without extra identifications. This rules out, for example,
   762	an icosahedron with a pair of opposite vertices identified.
   763	\end{enumerate}
   764	
   765	If $\sigma$ is clean, 
   766	$\link(s,\sigma)$ is clean.
   767	The boundary $\del \sigma$ of $\sigma$ is clean,
   768	and closed:
   769	$\del \del \sigma = \{\emptyset\}$.
   770	
   771	If $W$ is an $n$-manifold,
   772	say that a simplicial complex $\sigma$
   773	is a \emph{simplicial triangulation} of $W$
   774	if the geometric carrier of $\sigma$ is homeomorphic to $W$.
   775	In this case $\sigma$ is necessarily a clean complex.
   776	We imagine that we have prescribed a specific homeomorphism,
   777	at least up to the point of picking out one particular orientation
   778	$X(\sigma) \in C_n$ when $W$ is oriented;
   779	but we do not insist upon this, because nothing will depend on which
   780	orientation we pick.
   781	
   782	This notion of triangulation is not as general as you might want
   783	for some purposes.
   784	The double cover of a triangle is not a simplicial triangulation
   785	of $S^2$,
   786	because that would requires two distinct
   787	$2$-simplices to have the same three edges.
   788	Nor can any $2$-simplex have two of its edges
   789	glued to one another.
   790	Thurston
   791	\cite{thurston:shapes}
   792	allows such triangulations,
   793	but it is unclear how important these are to
   794	his theory of shapes of surfaces.
   795	We don't allow them.
   796	
   797	We continue to think of a chain $M \in C_n$ as a multiset of
   798	oriented $n$-simplices.
   799	We call $M$ \emph{simplicial} if no simplex is repeated, that is, if every
   800	simplex occurs in $M$ with multiplicity $0$ or $\pm 1$.
   801	In that case we write $K(M)$ for the \emph{support} of $M$: the pure
   802	simplicial complex generated by the $n$-simplices occurring in $M$.
   803	(In passing to $K(M)$ we view oriented simplices as a subclass of
   804	simplices, and identify a set of $n$-simplices with the pure $n$-complex
   805	it generates.)
   806	We call $M$ \emph{clean} if it is simplicial and $K(M)$ is clean.
   807	
   808	In particular, a filling $M$---an $(n\pls1)$-chain---is \emph{clean} when
   809	it has no repeated simplices and its support $K(M)$ is a clean simplicial
   810	complex. For a filling of a triangulated $2$-sphere, the simplices in
   811	question are tetrahedra.
   812	
   813	\section{Filling a triangulation of the 2-sphere}
   814	
   815	\begin{theorem} \label{th2}
   816	Let $\sigma$ be a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$,
   817	and $M$ a taut filling of $\sigma$.
   818	Then $M$ is clean, and its support $K(M)$
   819	is a simplicial triangulation of $B^3$.
   820	\end{theorem}
   821	
   822	\proofstart
   823	
   824	Let $v,e,f$  count the vertices, edges, and faces of $\sigma$.
   825	We have
   826	$e=3v-6$, $f=2v-4$.
   827	(Check: $3f=2e$, $v-e+f=2$.)
   828	
   829	Consider a counterexample pair $(\sigma,M)$ for which
   830	$\Zvol(\sigma)=|M|$ is minimal.
   831	Obviously $v>4$.
   832	We can assume that $\sigma$ is prime
   833	(not a connected sum along a triangle),
   834	and in particular (this is all we will need) that there is no
   835	vertex of degree $3$.
   836	
   837	Call a tet $t \in M$ \emph{eligible} if it shares two of its oriented
   838	faces with $\sigma$. (It cannot share more than two, since $\sigma$ has
   839	no vertex of degree $3$.)
   840	By Proposition \ref{maxdeg},
   841	\[
   842	|M| = \Zvol(\sigma) \leq f-\maxdeg(\sigma)
   843	.
   844	\]
   845	Regard $M$ as a multiset of tets, and assign to each $2$-simplex
   846	$\alpha$ of $\sigma$ some tet of $M$ having $\alpha$ as a properly
   847	oriented face. Such a tet exists, because $\alpha$ occurs with
   848	coefficient $+1$ in $\del M = X(\sigma)$, so some tet of $M$ contributes
   849	$\alpha$ with the correct sign. (Throughout we count support tets and
   850	correct boundary signs, not coefficient copies or cancelled appearances.)
   851	This assignment is at most $2$-to-$1$, since no tet of $M$ has more than
   852	two faces on $\sigma$, so at least $\maxdeg(\sigma)$ tets receive two
   853	faces each. These are \emph{disjointly eligible}: eligible tets whose
   854	pairs of boundary faces are mutually disjoint. We will need only two.
   855	
   856	Consider an eligible tet $t$ with boundary faces $s_1=[a,b,c]$ and
   857	$s_2=[b,a,d]$. Removing $t$ from $M$ produces a filling $M-t$ of a
   858	$2$-complex $\sigma_t$, in which $s_1,s_2$ have been replaced by the
   859	faces $[c,d,b],[d,c,a]$: the edge $ab$ has been \emph{flipped} to the
   860	edge $cd$. There are two cases.
   861	\begin{itemize}
   862	\item[(1)] Edge $cd$ was not already an edge of $\sigma$. Then
   863	$\sigma_t$ is again a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$, and $M-t$ is a
   864	taut filling of it. By minimality of $|M|$, $M-t$ is clean.
   865	\item[(2)] Edge $cd$ was already an edge of $\sigma$. Then $\sigma_t$
   866	is the almost disjoint union of two triangulations $\sigma_1,\sigma_2$
   867	of $S^2$, conjoined along the edge $cd$. By Theorem \ref{th1}, $M-t$
   868	splits as $M-t=M_1+M_2$, where each $M_i$ is a taut filling of
   869	$\sigma_i$; by minimality, each $M_i$ is clean. Here $M-t$ itself need
   870	\emph{not} be clean as a single complex---the link of the edge $cd$ in
   871	$K(M-t)$ is disconnected---even though it splits into the clean pieces
   872	$M_1,M_2$. It is, however, still simplicial.
   873	\end{itemize}
   874	In either case $M-t$ is simplicial.
   875	
   876	We must now show that $M$ itself is clean. First, $M$ is simplicial: the
   877	only way it could fail to be is for some eligible tet $t$ to occur in $M$
   878	with multiplicity $2$. Choose a second tet $u$, disjointly eligible from
   879	$t$, so that $u\neq t$. Removing $u$ leaves $2t$ present in $M-u$, so
   880	$M-u$ is not simplicial---contradicting the previous paragraph applied to
   881	$u$. Hence $M$ is simplicial.
   882	
   883	It remains to verify that $M$ is clean, by checking the three conditions
   884	defining a clean complex.
   885	
   886	\begin{enumerate}
   887	
   888	\item
   889	$M$ is a pseudomanifold.
   890	For suppose some $2$-simplex $s$ occurs more than twice as the boundary
   891	of a $3$-simplex of $M$.
   892	If $s \notin \sigma$,
   893	it must occur at least twice plus and twice minus;
   894	after removing any eligible tet it
   895	still occurs either twice plus or twice minus in $M-t$ in case (1),
   896	or in one or the other of $M_1,M_2$ in case (2),
   897	contradicting minimality.
   898	If $s \in \sigma$,
   899	it occurs at least twice plus in $M$:
   900	Remove some eligible tet that does not have $s$ as a face
   901	to get a contradiction.
   902	
   903	\item
   904	$M$ has no edge $\{a,b\}$ whose link
   905	is disconnected.
   906	For the link is a 1-dimensional complex whose edges correspond to
   907	tets of $M$ that contain $\{a,b\}$.
   908	The only way a component of the link can have fewer than three
   909	vertices is if consists of a single edge $\{c,d\}$, corresponding
   910	to a tet $t=\{a,b,c,d\} \in M$.
   911	In this case $\{a,b\}$ must be an edge of $\sigma$,
   912	and $\{a,b,c\},\{a,b,d\}$ the faces of $\sigma$
   913	that are adjacent along $\{a,b\}$.
   914	These are the only faces of $\sigma$ that contain $\{a,b\}$,
   915	so no other component of the link can reduce to a single edge.
   916	If the link is disconnected, removing any eligible tet
   917	other than $t$ will
   918	leave it disconnected, contradicting minimality.
   919	
   920	\item
   921	$M$ has no vertex whose link is disconnected:
   922	For if $\link(\{a\},M)$ is not connected,
   923	the only way removing a single tet $t$
   924	can render the link connected
   925	is if one component of the link has a single $2$-simplex
   926	$\{b,c,d\}$ and $t=\{a,b,c,d\}$.
   927	In this case $\{a,b,c\}$, $\{a,b,d\}$, $\{a,c,d\}$ must
   928	all be faces of $\sigma$,
   929	making $a$ a vertex of $\sigma$ of degree $3$, contradiction.
   930	
   931	\item Hence $M$ is clean.
   932	\proofend
   933	
   934	\end{enumerate}
   935	
   936	Thanks to this theorem we may, when convenient, speak of a taut filling
   937	$M$ of $\sigma$ as `being' the simplicial triangulation $\tau=K(M)$ of
   938	$B^3$.
   939	
   940	\section{Shelling}
   941	
   942	A \emph{shelling} of a simplicial triangulation $\tau$ of $B^3$ is an
   943	ordering $(t_1,\ldots,t_{|\tau|})$ of its tets such that gluing them on
   944	one at a time maintains a triangulation of $B^3$: for each
   945	$k=1,\ldots,|\tau|$, the subcomplex $\tau_k$ generated by
   946	$\{t_1,\ldots,t_k\}$ is a simplicial triangulation of $B^3$.
   947	For $k>1$ we classify $t_k$ by the number of faces it shares with
   948	$\tau_{k-1}$. Gluing along one face increases the vertex count $v$ by
   949	$1$; along two faces leaves $v$ unchanged; along three faces decreases
   950	$v$ by $1$. In our setting the third type never occurs, since gluing a
   951	tet along three faces would bury an interior vertex; thus $v$ never
   952	decreases. (A shelling with this property is sometimes called
   953	\emph{monotone}; here every shelling is monotone, so we do not belabor
   954	the distinction.)
   955	
   956	Call $\tau$ \emph{freely shellable} if any $t \in \tau$ can serve as the
   957	initial tet of a shelling.
   958	
   959	\begin{theorem} \label{th3}
   960	If $\sigma$ is a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$, then any taut filling
   961	$M$ of $\sigma$ is freely shellable: its support $\tau=K(M)$ is a
   962	simplicial triangulation of $B^3$ admitting a shelling that begins with
   963	any prescribed tet.
   964	\end{theorem}
   965	
   966	\proofstart
   967	Fix the designated initial tet $t_1 \in \tau$. Rather than building
   968	$\tau$ up starting from $t_1$, we describe a recursive procedure---call
   969	it \emph{shucking}---that takes $\tau$ apart so as to remove $t_1$ last;
   970	reversing it yields a shelling that begins with $t_1$.
   971	
   972	The base case is $\tau = \{t_1\}$.
   973	
   974	If $\tau$ has a vertex of degree $3$ lying on a tet $t \neq t_1$, remove
   975	$t$ and shuck $\tau-t$, ending with $t_1$.
   976	
   977	If $\tau$ has a vertex of degree $3$ lying on $t_1$, let $t_2$ be the tet
   978	of $\tau-t_1$ to which $t_1$ is attached; shuck $\tau-t_1$ ending with
   979	$t_2$, then remove $t_1$.
   980	
   981	If $\tau$ has no vertex of degree $3$, then, as in the proof of
   982	Theorem \ref{th2}, there are at least $\maxdeg \geq 4$ disjointly eligible
   983	tets. Pick one, $t$, sharing no face with $t_1$. If $\tau-t$ does not fall
   984	apart, remove $t$ and shuck $\tau-t$ ending with $t_1$. If $\tau-t$ falls
   985	apart into the almost disjoint union of $\tau_1$ and $\tau_2$, with
   986	$t_1 \in \tau_1$ and $t$ bridging the two pieces, let $t_2$ be the tet of
   987	$\tau_2$ to which $t$ attaches; shuck $\tau_2$ ending with $t_2$, remove
   988	$t$, then shuck $\tau_1$ ending with $t_1$.
   989	\proofend
   990	
   991	\section{Flag complex}
   992	
   993	A simplicial complex is a \emph{flag complex} (also called a
   994	\emph{clique complex}) if every clique in its $1$-skeleton occurs as a
   995	simplex.
   996	A simplicial triangulation $\sigma$ of $S^2$
   997	is flag just if it is \emph{prime} 
   998	(not the connected sum of smaller complexes)
   999	and not the boundary of a tetrahedron.
  1000	
  1001	We can decompose any $\sigma$ as the connected sum of
  1002	prime components $\sigma_i$.
  1003	(Of course, to reconstruct $\sigma$ we need more than this, we need to know
  1004	just how they are connected.)
  1005	From the point of taut fillings these components are independent:
  1006	Any taut filling $\tau$ of $\sigma$ is a union of taut fillings $\tau_i$.
  1007	That each of these is freely shellable implies the same of the union.
  1008	
  1009	Now we want to show that, whether or not $\sigma$ itself is prime, 
  1010	any taut filling of $\sigma$ is a flag complex.
  1011	
  1012	\begin{theorem}
  1013	If $\sigma$ is a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$,
  1014	any taut filling $\tau$ of $\sigma$ is a flag complex.
  1015	\end{theorem}
  1016	
  1017	\proofstart
  1018	The goal here is to show that neither of these
  1019	three taboo configurations occurs
  1020	in $\tau$:
  1021	\begin{enumerate}
  1022	\item
  1023	The three edges of triangle $\{A,B,C\}$ without the 2-simplex $ABC$.
  1024	\item
  1025	The six edges of the $K_4$ $\{A,B,C,D\}$ without the 3-simplex $ABCD$.
  1026	\item
  1027	The ten edges of the $K_5$ $\{A,B,C,D,E\}$.
  1028	\end{enumerate}
  1029	
  1030	If we can rule out 1 and 2 then 3 will follow, because
  1031	along with the $K_5$ we would have the five associated $3$-simplices:
  1032	Together these would form an $S^3$, not possible as a subcomplex
  1033	of the topological 3-ball $\tau$.
  1034	
  1035	To rule out 1 and 2,
  1036	the strategy will be to consider a minimal counterexample,
  1037	and find a modification that would produce a smaller counterexample.
  1038	
  1039	If $\sigma$ has a vertex of degree 3,
  1040	it is a connected sum with one summand a single tetrahedron.
  1041	We can pull off the vertex from $\sigma$, while removing the associated
  1042	tet from $\tau$, to produce a smaller counterexample.
  1043	
  1044	Since $\sigma$ has no vertex of degree $3$, there are at least
  1045	$\maxdeg \geq 4$ disjointly eligible tets: tets that each meet the
  1046	boundary in two faces, with these pairs of faces disjoint.
  1047	Removing any one of these tets performs an `edge flip' on the boundary
  1048	$\sigma$,
  1049	which either (a) remains a triangulation of the 2-sphere,
  1050	or (b) becomes an almost disjoint union where
  1051	two such triangulations are joined along an edge.
  1052	We will show that some choice for the flip does not remove the taboo
  1053	configuration.
  1054	In case (a) we have a smaller counterexample already.
  1055	In case (b) the key is that if a $K_3$ or $K_4$ occurs
  1056	in the almost disjoint union, it occurs on one side or the other,
  1057	also giving a counterexample.
  1058	
  1059	Configuration 1:
  1060	When we remove a tet from $\tau$ to flip an edge of $\sigma$,
  1061	the only edge that disappears from $\tau$ is the edge that gets flipped.
  1062	There are four choices of the edge to flip, and three edges we must
  1063	avoid flipping.
  1064	
  1065	Configuration 2:
  1066	Now there are six edges we must avoid flipping. Having dealt with
  1067	configuration 1, we know that along with the six edges of $\{A,B,C,D\}$,
  1068	$\tau$ contains all four faces $ABC$, $ABD$, $ACD$, $BCD$. At most two of
  1069	these can be boundary faces, since $\sigma$ has no vertex of degree $3$;
  1070	so at most five of the six $K_4$ edges lie on the boundary. Treating the
  1071	octahedron separately as a special case, we may assume $\maxdeg \geq 5$,
  1072	which gives at least five boundary edges available to flip. The only
  1073	potential obstruction is when exactly five of the six $K_4$ edges lie on
  1074	the boundary, the interior edge being, say, $AD$; then the two boundary
  1075	faces of the $K_4$ are $ABC$ and $BCD$. Removing an eligible tet does no
  1076	harm unless one of its two boundary faces is $ABC$ or $BCD$. At most two
  1077	of our disjointly eligible tets can have $ABC$ or $BCD$ as a boundary
  1078	face, leaving at least $\maxdeg-2 \geq 3$ further eligible tets whose
  1079	removal flips an edge while preserving the $K_4$.
  1080	\proofend
  1081	
  1082	\bibliography{taut}
  1083	\bibliographystyle{amsplain}
  1084	\end{document}

