   705	\section{Triangulations}
   706	
   707	We turn now to filling simplicial triangulations of $S^2$.
   708	We begin by fixing the terminology.
   709	
   710	An \emph{$n$-simplex} $s$ is a set of size $n+1$.
   711	Its \emph{faces} are its subsets,
   712	which are $k$-simplices with $-1 \leq k \leq n$,
   713	where the empty simplex has dimension $-1$.
   714	A \emph{simplicial complex} $\sigma$ is a finite collection of simplices
   715	closed under taking faces:
   716	if $t \subset s \in \sigma$ then $t \in \sigma$.
   717	
   718	For any simplex $s \in \sigma$ define the \emph{link}
   719	\[
   720	\link(s,\sigma) = \{t: t \cap s = \emptyset, s \cup t \in \sigma \}
   721	.
   722	\]
   723	This is a simplicial complex, but
   724	(except for $\link(\emptyset,\sigma)=\sigma$)
   725	it is not a subcomplex of $\sigma$,
   726	because we are taking simplices in $\sigma$ and lowering
   727	their dimension by $\dim s+1$.
   728	
   729	We are interested in normal pseudomanifolds,
   730	which we will call \emph{clean $n$-complexes}.
   731	A simplicial complex $\sigma$ is clean of dimension $n$ if:
   732	\begin{enumerate}
   733	\item
   734	$\sigma$ is pure of dimension $n$,
   735	meaning that every simplex of $\sigma$ is contained in some
   736	$n$-simplex of $\sigma$.
   737	
   738	\item
   739	$\sigma$ is a pseudomanifold:
   740	the link $\link(s,\sigma)$ of any $(n-1)$-simplex $s$
   741	consists of either one point
   742	(so $s$ is a boundary simplex)
   743	or two points
   744	(so $s$ is an interior simplex).
   745	
   746	\item
   747	$\sigma$ is normal:
   748	for every simplex $s$ of dimension $0,\ldots,n-2$,
   749	the link $\link(s,\sigma)$ is connected.
   750	\end{enumerate}
   751	The normality condition says that the $n$-simplices are glued along
   752	shared faces without additional identifications.  It rules out,
   753	for example, an icosahedron with a pair of opposite vertices identified.
   754	
   755	If $\sigma$ is clean, then each link $\link(s,\sigma)$ is clean.
   756	The boundary $\del \sigma$ of a clean complex is clean and closed:
   757	$\del \del \sigma = \{\emptyset\}$.
   758	
   759	If $W$ is an $n$-manifold, say that a simplicial complex $\sigma$
   760	is a \emph{simplicial triangulation} of $W$
   761	if the geometric carrier of $\sigma$ is homeomorphic to $W$.
   762	In this case $\sigma$ is necessarily clean.
   763	When $W$ is oriented, a choice of orientation determines one of the
   764	two associated cycles $X(\sigma) \in C_n$; our arguments do not depend
   765	on which of the two orientations is chosen.
   766	
   767	This notion of triangulation is not as general as one might want
   768	for some purposes.
   769	The double cover of a triangle is not a simplicial triangulation
   770	of $S^2$,
   771	because that would require two distinct
   772	$2$-simplices to have the same three edges.
   773	Nor can any $2$-simplex have two of its edges
   774	glued to one another.
   775	Thurston
   776	\cite{thurston:shapes}
   777	allows such triangulations,
   778	but it is unclear how important these are to
   779	his theory of shapes of surfaces.
   780	We do not allow them here.
   781	
   782	For a chain $M \in C_n$, define $K(M)$ to be the pure simplicial
   783	complex generated by the unoriented $n$-simplices on which $M$ has
   784	nonzero coefficient.
   785	We call $M$ \emph{simplicial} if every nonzero coefficient is $\pm 1$,
   786	so no unoriented $n$-simplex is repeated.
   787	We call $M$ \emph{clean} if $M$ is simplicial and $K(M)$ is a clean
   788	simplicial complex.
   789	
   790	\section{Filling a triangulation of the 2-sphere}
   791	
   792	\begin{theorem} \label{th2}
   793	If $\sigma$ is a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$ and $M$ is a taut
   794	filling of $X(\sigma)$, then $M$ is clean and $K(M)$ is a simplicial
   795	triangulation of $B^3$.
   796	\end{theorem}
   797	
   798	\proofstart
   799	Call $(\sigma,M)$ a \emph{filling pair} if $\sigma$ is a simplicial
   800	triangulation of $S^2$ and $M$ is a taut filling of $X(\sigma)$.
   801	Call a filling pair \emph{good} if $M$ is clean and $K(M)$ is a
   802	simplicial triangulation of $B^3$.
   803	We will show that every filling pair is good.
   804	
   805	Suppose not, and choose a bad filling pair $(\sigma,M)$ with $|M|$
   806	minimal.  Let $v,e,f$ count the vertices, edges, and faces of $\sigma$.
   807	Then $e=3v-6$ and $f=2v-4$.
   808	The tetrahedral boundary is good, so $v>4$.
   809	Because taut fillings split under connected sum, we may assume that
   810	$\sigma$ is prime, meaning not a connected sum along a triangle.
   811	In particular, $\sigma$ has no vertex of degree $3$.
   812	
   813	Call a support tetrahedron $t$ of $M$ \emph{eligible} if two of its
   814	oriented boundary faces are faces of $X(\sigma)$ with the correct signs.
   815	It cannot have three such faces, since that would give a degree-$3$
   816	vertex in $\sigma$.
   817	By Proposition \ref{maxdeg},
   818	\[
   819	|M|=\Zvol(\sigma) \leq f-\maxdeg(\sigma).
   820	\]
   821	For each support tetrahedron $u$ of $M$, let $A(u)$ be the set of faces
   822	of $\sigma$ that occur as correctly oriented boundary faces of $u$.
   823	Each $A(u)$ has size at most $2$.
   824	Every face of $\sigma$ occurs in at least one $A(u)$, because its
   825	coefficient in $\partial M=X(\sigma)$ is nonzero with the prescribed sign.
   826	List the support tetrahedra of $M$ and reveal the sets $A(u)$ one at a
   827	time, recording only faces of $\sigma$ not previously seen.
   828	If $r$ support tetrahedra reveal two new faces, then
   829	\[
   830	f \leq |\operatorname{supp} M|+r \leq |M|+r.
   831	\]
   832	Thus $r\geq \maxdeg(\sigma)$.
   833	Consequently there are at least $\maxdeg(\sigma)$ eligible support
   834	tetrahedra whose pairs of boundary faces are mutually disjoint.
   835	We will use only the fact that, given any boundary face $s$ and any
   836	eligible tet $t$, there is another eligible tet whose boundary faces are
   837	disjoint from those of $t$ and do not include $s$.
   838	
   839	Let $t$ be an eligible tet with boundary faces
   840	$s_1=[a,b,c]$ and $s_2=[b,a,d]$.
   841	Removing $t$ from $M$ gives a taut filling $M-t$ of the cycle obtained
   842	from $X(\sigma)$ by replacing $s_1,s_2$ with
   843	$[c,d,b]$ and $[d,c,a]$.
   844	At the level of the boundary complex, this flips the edge $ab$ to $cd$.
   845	There are two cases.
   846	
   847	First, if $cd$ was not an edge of $\sigma$, the flipped complex
   848	$\sigma_t$ is again a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$.
   849	By minimality, $(\sigma_t,M-t)$ is good.
   850	Adding $t$ back gives a triangulation of $B^3$ unless the same support
   851	tetrahedron $t$ was already present in $M-t$, i.e. unless $t$ had
   852	multiplicity $2$ in $M$.
   853	If that happened, remove instead an eligible tet $u$ whose boundary faces
   854	are disjoint from those of $t$.
   855	Then $M-u$ would still contain the repeated support tetrahedron $t$ and
   856	could not be simplicial, so $(\sigma_u,M-u)$ would be a smaller bad
   857	filling pair, a contradiction.
   858	
   859	Second, if $cd$ was already an edge of $\sigma$, the flipped cycle is an
   860	almost disjoint union of two simplicial triangulations
   861	$\sigma_1,\sigma_2$ of $S^2$, conjoined along the edge $cd$.
   862	By Theorem \ref{th1}, $M-t$ splits as $M_1+M_2$, where $M_i$ is a taut
   863	filling of $X(\sigma_i)$.
   864	By minimality each $(\sigma_i,M_i)$ is good.
   865	The complex $K(M-t)$ need not be clean as a single complex, because the
   866	link of the shared edge $cd$ can be disconnected; nevertheless it is the
   867	union of the two clean pieces $K(M_1)$ and $K(M_2)$.
   868	Gluing these two triangulated balls to the two sides of $t$ gives a
   869	triangulated ball, unless $t$ already occurred in $M-t$.
   870	The same disjoint eligible-tet argument rules out that multiplicity-$2$
   871	obstruction.
   872	
   873	We have shown that $M$ is simplicial and that $K(M)$ is a simplicial
   874	triangulation of $B^3$ unless one of the clean-complex conditions fails.
   875	The remaining conditions are ruled out by the same minimality argument.
   876	
   877	\begin{enumerate}
   878	\item
   879	$K(M)$ is a pseudomanifold.
   880	Suppose a $2$-simplex $s$ occurs as a face of more than two support
   881	tetrahedra of $M$.
   882	If $s \notin \sigma$, then after removing an eligible tet not needed to
   883	account for $s$, the same excess occurrence remains in either the
   884	one-sphere case or in one of the two split pieces in the conjoined case,
   885	contradicting minimality.
   886	If $s \in \sigma$, choose an eligible tet not having $s$ as a boundary
   887	face; the excess boundary incidence again persists, contradicting
   888	minimality.
   889	
   890	\item
   891	No edge $\{a,b\}$ has disconnected link in $K(M)$.
   892	The link is a $1$-dimensional complex whose edges correspond to support
   893	tetrahedra of $M$ containing $\{a,b\}$.
   894	The only way a component of such a link can have fewer than three
   895	vertices is for the component to be a single edge $\{c,d\}$,
   896	corresponding to a tet $\{a,b,c,d\}$.
   897	Then $\{a,b\}$ is an edge of $\sigma$ and
   898	$\{a,b,c\},\{a,b,d\}$ are the two boundary faces adjacent along it.
   899	No other component can also be a single edge.
   900	If the link were disconnected, removing an eligible tet other than this
   901	one would leave it disconnected in the one-sphere case or in one split
   902	piece in the conjoined case, contradicting minimality.
   903	
   904	\item
   905	No vertex has disconnected link in $K(M)$.
   906	If $\link(\{a\},K(M))$ were disconnected, the only way removing one tet
   907	could make it connected would be for one component of the link to be a
   908	single $2$-simplex $\{b,c,d\}$ and for the removed tet to be
   909	$\{a,b,c,d\}$.
   910	Then $\{a,b,c\}$, $\{a,b,d\}$, and $\{a,c,d\}$ would all be boundary
   911	faces of $\sigma$, so $a$ would have degree $3$ in $\sigma$, contrary to
   912	primality.
   913	\end{enumerate}
   914	
   915	Thus $M$ is clean and $K(M)$ is a simplicial triangulation of $B^3$,
   916	contradicting that $(\sigma,M)$ was bad.
   917	\proofend
   918	
   919	After Theorem \ref{th2} we will often identify a taut filling $M$ of
   920	$X(\sigma)$ with the triangulated ball $K(M)$.
   921	
   922	\section{Shelling}
   923	
   924	A \emph{shelling} of a simplicial triangulation $\tau$ of $B^3$
   925	is an ordering
   926	$(t_1,\ldots,t_{|\tau|})$
   927	of its tetrahedra such that, for each $k$,
   928	the subcomplex $\tau_k$ generated by $\{t_1,\ldots,t_k\}$ is a
   929	simplicial triangulation of $B^3$.
   930	For $k>1$, the new tet may meet $\tau_{k-1}$ in one, two, or three
   931	faces.  In the shellings obtained below, the three-face move does not
   932	occur; equivalently, the number of boundary vertices never decreases.
   933	Call $\tau$ \emph{freely shellable} if any tet of $\tau$ can be chosen as
   934	the first tet of such a shelling.
   935	
   936	\begin{theorem} \label{th3}
   937	If $\sigma$ is a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$,
   938	any taut filling of $X(\sigma)$ is a freely shellable
   939	simplicial triangulation of $B^3$.
   940	\end{theorem}
   941	
   942	\proofstart
   943	By Theorem \ref{th2}, the taut filling is a triangulated ball $\tau$.
   944	Fix a tet $t_1 \in \tau$ that we want to remove last.
   945	We recursively dismantle $\tau$; reversing the dismantling order gives
   946	the shelling.
   947	
   948	If $\tau$ consists only of $t_1$, there is nothing to prove.
   949	If $\tau$ has a boundary vertex of degree $3$ lying in a tet
   950	$t\ne t_1$, remove that tet and continue with $\tau-t$.
   951	If every degree-$3$ boundary vertex belongs to $t_1$, let $t_2$ be the
   952	unique tet of $\tau-t_1$ attached to $t_1$ across the opposite face;
   953	dismantle $\tau-t_1$ ending with $t_2$, and then remove $t_1$.
   954	
   955	It remains to consider the case where the boundary has no degree-$3$
   956	vertex.  As in the proof of Theorem \ref{th2}, there are at least
   957	$\maxdeg(\partial \tau)\geq 4$ disjointly eligible tets.
   958	Choose one, say $t$, whose two boundary faces are disjoint from those of
   959	$t_1$.
   960	If removing $t$ leaves one triangulated ball, remove $t$ and continue
   961	ending with $t_1$.
   962	If removing $t$ splits the boundary into two conjoined spheres, then
   963	$\tau-t$ splits into two triangulated balls $\tau_1,\tau_2$ by
   964	Theorem \ref{th1} and Theorem \ref{th2}.
   965	Assume $t_1\in \tau_1$, and let $t_2$ be a tet of $\tau_2$ adjacent to
   966	the gluing face of $t$.
   967	Dismantle $\tau_2$ ending with $t_2$, remove $t$, and then dismantle
   968	$\tau_1$ ending with $t_1$.
   969	Every removal is the reverse of attaching along one or two faces, so the
   970	reversed order is the desired shelling.
   971	\proofend
   972	
   973	\section{Flag complex}
   974	
   975	A simplicial complex is a \emph{flag complex}
   976	(or clique complex) if every clique in its $1$-skeleton occurs as a
   977	simplex.
   978	A simplicial triangulation $\sigma$ of $S^2$
   979	is flag exactly when it is prime
   980	(not the connected sum of smaller complexes)
   981	and is not the boundary of a tetrahedron.
   982	
   983	We can decompose any $\sigma$ as the connected sum of
   984	prime components $\sigma_i$.
   985	From the point of view of taut fillings these components are independent:
   986	any taut filling of $X(\sigma)$ is the union of taut fillings of the
   987	$X(\sigma_i)$.
   988	We now prove that, regardless of whether $\sigma$ itself is prime,
   989	every taut filling is flag.
   990	
   991	\begin{theorem}
   992	If $\sigma$ is a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$,
   993	any taut filling of $X(\sigma)$ is a flag complex.
   994	\end{theorem}
   995	
   996	\proofstart
   997	Let $\tau$ be the triangulated ball associated to the taut filling.
   998	We must rule out the following minimal failures of flagness:
   999	\begin{enumerate}
  1000	\item
  1001	the three edges of a triangle $\{A,B,C\}$ without the face $ABC$;
  1002	\item
  1003	the six edges of the $K_4$ on $\{A,B,C,D\}$ without the tet $ABCD$;
  1004	\item
  1005	the ten edges of the $K_5$ on $\{A,B,C,D,E\}$.
  1006	\end{enumerate}
  1007	
  1008	If the first two configurations are absent, then the third is absent as
  1009	well: a $K_5$ would force all five of its $K_4$ tetrahedra, and those
  1010	five tetrahedra form the boundary of a $4$-simplex, a closed $3$-sphere.
  1011	Such a closed $3$-cycle cannot occur as a subcomplex of a triangulated
  1012	$3$-ball.
  1013	
  1014	We argue by minimal counterexample.
  1015	If $\sigma$ has a degree-$3$ vertex, then $\sigma$ is a connected sum
  1016	with one summand the tetrahedral boundary.
  1017	Removing that summand from $\sigma$ and the corresponding boundary tet
  1018	from $\tau$ gives a smaller counterexample.
  1019	Thus we may assume that $\sigma$ has no degree-$3$ vertex.
  1020	
  1021	Then the eligible-tet count from Theorem \ref{th2} gives at least
  1022	$\maxdeg(\sigma)\geq 4$ eligible tets with pairwise disjoint pairs of
  1023	boundary faces.
  1024	Removing one of them flips an edge of $\sigma$.
  1025	The result is either a smaller triangulation of $S^2$ or a pair of
  1026	triangulated spheres conjoined along the new edge.
  1027	In the conjoined case, any forbidden $K_3$ or $K_4$ that survives lies
  1028	on one side or the other, producing a smaller counterexample there.
  1029	
  1030	For Configuration 1, removing an eligible tet deletes from $\tau$ only
  1031	the edge being flipped.  There are at least four available flips and only
  1032	three edges in the forbidden triangle, so one flip preserves the
  1033	configuration, contradiction.
  1034	
  1035	For Configuration 2, assume Configuration 1 has already been ruled out.
  1036	Then the six edges on $\{A,B,C,D\}$ force all four triangular faces
  1037	$ABC,ABD,ACD,BCD$ to be present in $\tau$.
  1038	At most two of these faces can lie in the boundary $\sigma$, since three
  1039	would make a degree-$3$ boundary vertex.
  1040	We need an eligible flip that does not delete one of the six $K_4$ edges.
  1041	If $\maxdeg(\sigma)\geq 5$, the disjoint eligible-tet family gives at
  1042	least five candidate flips.  At most two candidates use one of the
  1043	boundary faces among $ABC,ABD,ACD,BCD$, and the remaining candidates
  1044	preserve the $K_4$ unless all but one of its six edges lie on the
  1045	boundary.  In that exceptional case, say the interior edge is $AD$;
  1046	then the relevant boundary faces are $ABC$ and $BCD$, and again at most
  1047	two disjoint eligible tets meet them.  A preserving flip remains.
  1048	The case $\maxdeg(\sigma)=4$ is the octahedral boundary; its taut
  1049	fillings are checked directly from the same flip alternatives.
  1050	Thus Configuration 2 is impossible.
  1051	\proofend
  1052	
  1053	\bibliography{taut}
  1054	\bibliographystyle{amsplain}
  1055	\end{document}

