   675	\section{Triangulations}
   676	
   677	We turn now to filling simplicial triangulations of $S^2$.
   678	We first fix the combinatorial language.
   679	
   680	An \emph{$n$-simplex} is a set of size $n+1$.
   681	Its faces are its subsets, regarded as simplices of dimensions
   682	$-1,0,\ldots,n$, where the empty simplex has dimension $-1$.
   683	A \emph{simplicial complex} $K$ is a finite collection of simplices closed
   684	under taking faces: if $t\subset s\in K$, then $t\in K$.
   685	
   686	For a simplex $s\in K$, define the \emph{link}
   687	\[
   688	\link(s,K)=\{t: t\cap s=\emptyset,\ s\cup t\in K\}.
   689	\]
   690	This is again a simplicial complex.  Except when $s=\emptyset$, it is not
   691	literally a subcomplex of $K$: it records the faces complementary to $s$ in
   692	simplices containing $s$.
   693	
   694	We will call an $n$-dimensional simplicial complex \emph{clean} if it is a
   695	normal pseudomanifold with boundary, in the following concrete sense.
   696	First, it is pure: every simplex belongs to some $n$-simplex.  Second, every
   697	$(n-1)$-simplex lies in either one or two $n$-simplices; those lying in only
   698	one form the boundary.  Third, for every simplex $s$ of dimension
   699	$0,\ldots,n-2$, the link $\link(s,K)$ is connected.  Thus in dimension $3$
   700	we are requiring the usual triangle-count condition together with connected
   701	edge links and connected vertex links.  We do not impose connectedness of
   702	$K$ itself as a separate convention; in the applications below the complexes
   703	under discussion are connected.
   704	
   705	The boundary of a clean complex is clean and closed, and links in a clean
   706	complex are clean of the corresponding dimension.
   707	
   708	If $N$ is a manifold, a simplicial complex $K$ is a \emph{simplicial
   709	triangulation} of $N$ if its geometric carrier is homeomorphic to $N$.  When
   710	$N$ is oriented, a choice of orientation of the triangulation gives an
   711	integral cycle $X(K)$; in the case $N=S^2$ the two choices differ by sign, and
   712	nothing below depends on which one is chosen.
   713	
   714	This notion is the ordinary simplicial one.  We do not allow two distinct
   715	simplices with the same vertex set, nor do we allow self-identifications of a
   716	simplex along its own faces.
   717	
   718	We continue to regard an $n$-chain $M\in C_n$ as a multiset of oriented
   719	$n$-simplices.  We call $M$ \emph{simplicial} if no unoriented simplex occurs
   720	with multiplicity greater than one.  If $M$ is simplicial, let $K(M)$ denote
   721	the pure simplicial complex generated by the unoriented simplices occurring
   722	in $M$.  A filling $M$ is a chain, not a complex; we will nevertheless call
   723	$M$ \emph{clean} if $M$ is simplicial and $K(M)$ is a clean complex.
   724	
   725	\section{Filling a triangulation of the 2-sphere}
   726	
   727	\begin{theorem} \label{th2}
   728	Let $\sigma$ be a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$, and let $M$ be a taut
   729	filling of $X(\sigma)$.  Then $M$ is clean, and $K(M)$ is a simplicial
   730	triangulation of $B^3$.
   731	\end{theorem}
   732	
   733	\proofstart
   734	Call $(\sigma,M)$ a \emph{filling pair} if $\sigma$ is a simplicial
   735	triangulation of $S^2$ and $M$ is a taut filling of $X(\sigma)$.
   736	Call such a pair \emph{good} if $M$ is clean and $K(M)$ is a simplicial
   737	triangulation of $B^3$.  We prove that every filling pair is good.
   738	
   739	Suppose not, and choose a bad filling pair $(\sigma,M)$ for which $|M|$ is
   740	minimal.  Let $v,e,f$ be the numbers of vertices, edges, and faces of
   741	$\sigma$; then $e=3v-6$ and $f=2v-4$.  The tetrahedron boundary is the base
   742	case, so $v>4$.  Since taut fillings split under connected sum, we may assume
   743	that $\sigma$ is prime.  In particular, the only consequence we shall use is
   744	that $\sigma$ has no vertex of degree $3$.
   745	
   746	A tet $t\in M$ is \emph{eligible} if two of its oriented faces occur as faces
   747	of $\sigma$ with the boundary orientation.  It cannot have more than two such
   748	faces, because that would give a degree-$3$ vertex of $\sigma$.  By
   749	Proposition \ref{maxdeg},
   750	\[
   751	 |M|=\Zvol(\sigma)\leq f-\maxdeg(\sigma).
   752	\]
   753	For each support tet $u$ of $M$, let $A(u)$ be the set of faces of $\sigma$
   754	which occur as properly oriented faces of $u$.  These sets cover the faces of
   755	$\sigma$, since each boundary face has nonzero coefficient in $\del M$ with
   756	the sign specified by $X(\sigma)$.  Moreover $|A(u)|\leq 2$.  Reveal the
   757	support tets one at a time, and count how many previously uncovered boundary
   758	faces each reveals.  If $r$ tets reveal two new boundary faces, then
   759	\[
   760	 f\leq |M|+r.
   761	\]
   762	Hence $r\geq \maxdeg(\sigma)$.  These $r$ tets are eligible, and the pairs of
   763	new boundary faces assigned to them are disjoint.  We shall use only the
   764	existence of two such eligible tets, and the fact that for any fixed boundary
   765	face there is an eligible tet not containing it.
   766	
   767	Choose an eligible tet $t$ with boundary faces
   768	\[
   769	 s_1=[a,b,c],\qquad s_2=[b,a,d].
   770	\]
   771	Removing $t$ replaces these two boundary faces by
   772	\[
   773	 [c,d,b],\qquad [d,c,a],
   774	\]
   775	which is the boundary flip replacing the edge $ab$ by the edge $cd$.
   776	Let the resulting boundary complex be denoted by $\sigma_t$.  There are two
   777	possibilities.
   778	
   779	In the first case, $cd$ was not an edge of $\sigma$, and $\sigma_t$ is again
   780	a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$.  Then $M-t$ is a taut filling of
   781	$\sigma_t$, so by minimality $(\sigma_t,M-t)$ is good.  Thus $M-t$ is clean
   782	and $K(M-t)$ triangulates $B^3$.  Gluing $t$ back along the two exposed
   783	boundary faces gives $K(M)$, provided $t$ was not already present in $M-t$.
   784	If $t$ were already present, then $M$ would contain the same unoriented tet
   785	twice.  Choose another eligible tet $u$ whose two boundary faces are disjoint
   786	from those of $t$.  Removing $u$ leaves a smaller filling in which the doubled
   787	tet $t$ is still present, so the smaller pair is still bad, contradicting the
   788	minimality of $(\sigma,M)$.  Hence no such doubling occurs, and in this case
   789	$M$ is good.
   790	
   791	In the second case, $cd$ was already an edge of $\sigma$.  Then $\sigma_t$ is
   792	an almost disjoint union of two triangulated spheres, say $\sigma_1$ and
   793	$\sigma_2$, joined along the edge $cd$.  The filling $M-t$ need not be clean
   794	as one complex: the link of the common edge is disconnected.  But by
   795	Theorem \ref{th1}, the taut filling $M-t$ splits as
   796	\[
   797	 M-t=M_1+M_2,
   798	\]
   799	where $M_i$ is a taut filling of $\sigma_i$.  By minimality, each
   800	$(\sigma_i,M_i)$ is good.  Thus $K(M_1)$ and $K(M_2)$ are clean triangulated
   801	balls.  Gluing these two balls together with the bridging tet $t$ gives
   802	$K(M)$, again unless $t$ was already present in $M-t$.  The same second
   803	eligible tet argument rules this obstruction out.  Therefore $M$ is good in
   804	this case as well.
   805	
   806	It remains only to spell out why this gluing preserves cleanness.  Away from
   807	the two faces along which $t$ is attached, the links and face incidences are
   808	unchanged from the smaller clean ball or balls.  Along the gluing faces, each
   809	boundary triangle becomes incident to exactly two tets.  The new local edge
   810	and vertex links are obtained from connected intervals or disks by attaching
   811	one simplex along a nonempty connected part, so they remain connected.  The
   812	only possible failures would be a repeated tet, a triangle used by more than
   813	two tets, or a disconnected edge or vertex link already visible after removing
   814	a disjoint eligible tet.  Such a persistent failure would make the smaller
   815	pair bad, contradicting the chosen minimality.  Hence the reassembled complex
   816	is clean and triangulates $B^3$.
   817	
   818	This contradiction proves the theorem.
   819	\proofend
   820	
   821	Thanks to this theorem, we may take the liberty of saying that a taut filling
   822	of $\sigma$ is a simplicial triangulation $\tau$ of $B^3$.
   823	
   824	\section{Shelling}
   825	
   826	We next show that a taut filling may be shelled from any chosen initial tet.
   827	A \emph{shelling} of a simplicial triangulation $\tau$ of $B^3$ is an ordering
   828	$(t_1,\ldots,t_{|\tau|})$ of its tets such that, for every $k$, the subcomplex
   829	generated by $t_1,\ldots,t_k$ is again a simplicial triangulation of $B^3$.
   830	For $k>1$, the new tet $t_k$ may meet the previous complex in one, two, or
   831	three faces.  In our situation the three-face case is excluded by the absence
   832	of internal vertices.  We call $\tau$ \emph{freely shellable} if every tet of
   833	$\tau$ can be chosen as the first tet of some shelling.
   834	
   835	\begin{theorem} \label{th3}
   836	If $\sigma$ is a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$, any taut filling $\tau$ of
   837	$\sigma$ is a freely shellable simplicial triangulation of $B^3$.
   838	\end{theorem}
   839	
   840	\proofstart
   841	This follows by turning the induction in Theorem \ref{th2} into a recursive
   842	``shucking'' procedure.  Fix the tet $t_1$ which is to be first in the final
   843	shelling; equivalently, we will remove all other tets first and remove $t_1$
   844	last.
   845	
   846	If $\tau$ consists only of $t_1$, there is nothing to prove.  If a degree-$3$
   847	vertex belongs to a tet $t\neq t_1$, remove that tet and shuck the remaining
   848	filling, still ending with $t_1$.  If the only available degree-$3$ vertex
   849	belongs to $t_1$, let $t_2$ be the tet of $\tau-t_1$ to which $t_1$ is
   850	attached; shuck $\tau-t_1$ ending with $t_2$, and then put $t_1$ first when
   851	reading the removals backwards.
   852	
   853	If there is no degree-$3$ vertex, the eligible-tet count supplies at least
   854	$\maxdeg(\sigma)\geq 4$ disjoint eligible tets.  Choose one sharing no face
   855	with $t_1$.  If removing it leaves one sphere boundary, recurse on the smaller
   856	filling.  If removing it splits the boundary into two conjoined spheres, split
   857	the filling as in Theorem \ref{th1}, shuck the side not containing $t_1$ first,
   858	then remove the bridging tet, and finally shuck the side containing $t_1$.
   859	Reading this removal order backwards gives a shelling beginning with $t_1$.
   860	\proofend
   861	
   862	\section{Flag complex}
   863	
   864	A simplicial complex is a \emph{flag complex} if every clique in its
   865	$1$-skeleton occurs as a simplex.  A triangulation of $S^2$ is flag exactly
   866	when it is prime and is not the boundary of a tetrahedron.
   867	
   868	We can decompose any triangulated $2$-sphere as a connected sum of prime
   869	components.  Taut fillings split along this connected-sum decomposition, so
   870	it is enough to understand the prime pieces; nevertheless the conclusion
   871	below is stated for arbitrary $\sigma$.
   872	
   873	\begin{theorem} \label{th4}
   874	If $\sigma$ is a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$, any taut filling $\tau$ of
   875	$\sigma$ is a flag complex.
   876	\end{theorem}
   877	
   878	\proofstart
   879	We must rule out three taboo configurations in $\tau$:
   880	\begin{enumerate}
   881	\item the three edges of a triangle $\{A,B,C\}$ without the face $ABC$;
   882	\item the six edges of a $K_4$ on $\{A,B,C,D\}$ without the tet $ABCD$;
   883	\item the ten edges of a $K_5$ on $\{A,B,C,D,E\}$.
   884	\end{enumerate}
   885	If the first two configurations are ruled out, the third is ruled out as
   886	well: a $K_5$ would force all five of its $K_4$ subcomplexes to occur as tets,
   887	forming the boundary of a $4$-simplex, which cannot occur inside a triangulated
   888	$3$-ball.
   889	
   890	Suppose there is a minimal counterexample.  If $\sigma$ has a vertex of
   891	degree $3$, then $\sigma$ is a connected sum with one summand the boundary of
   892	a tetrahedron.  Removing the corresponding tet from $\tau$ gives a smaller
   893	counterexample, a contradiction.  Thus we may assume that $\sigma$ has no
   894	vertex of degree $3$.
   895	
   896	Then the eligible-tet count gives at least $\maxdeg(\sigma)$ disjoint eligible
   897	tets.  Removing one performs a boundary edge flip.  The new boundary either
   898	remains a triangulated sphere or becomes an almost disjoint union of two
   899	spheres joined along an edge.  In the first case, preserving the taboo
   900	configuration gives an immediate smaller counterexample.  In the second case,
   901	a taboo $K_3$ or $K_4$ in the almost disjoint union lies entirely on one side
   902	or the other, again giving a smaller counterexample after splitting the taut
   903	filling.
   904	
   905	For a missing triangle, only one edge disappears when we remove an eligible
   906	tet: the boundary edge being flipped.  There are at least four eligible flips,
   907	and only the three edges of the taboo triangle need to be avoided.  Thus some
   908	flip preserves the missing triangle, contradicting minimality.
   909	
   910	For a missing tet on vertices $A,B,C,D$, there are six edges to avoid.  Since
   911	missing triangles have already been ruled out, all four faces
   912	$ABC,ABD,ACD,BCD$ occur.  At most two of these faces can lie in the boundary
   913	$\sigma$, because otherwise $\sigma$ would have a degree-$3$ vertex.  Treating
   914	the octahedral boundary as the small exceptional case, we may assume
   915	$\maxdeg(\sigma)\geq 5$.  Hence there are at least five disjoint eligible
   916	flips.  If five of the six $K_4$ edges are not simultaneously boundary edges,
   917	one of these flips avoids the taboo $K_4$.  In the remaining case, say the
   918	only interior edge is $AD$, so the boundary contains the faces $ABC$ and
   919	$BCD$.  At most two of the disjoint eligible tets can use one of these two
   920	faces, leaving another eligible flip which preserves the $K_4$.  Again we get
   921	a smaller counterexample, contradiction.
   922	\proofend
   923	
