   164	\begin{document}
   165	
   166	\maketitle
   167	
   168	\begin{abstract}
   169	Let $\sigma$ be a simplicial triangulation of the 2-sphere,
   170	$X$ the associated integral 2-cycle.
   171	A filling of $X$ is an integral 3-chain $M$
   172	with $\del M = X$;
   173	a taut filling is one with minimal $L_1$-norm.
   174	We show that any taut filling 
   175	arises from an extension of $\sigma$ to
   176	a simplicial complex homeomorphic to the 3-ball.
   177	This complex is shellable, and `flag':
   178	every clique in its 1-skeleton occurs as a simplex.
   179	The key to the proof is the general
   180	fact that any taut filling of an $n$-cycle
   181	splits under disjoint union, connected sum, and more generally
   182	what we call almost disjoint union,
   183	where summands are supported on sets that overlap in at most $n+1$ vertices.
   184	\begin{comment}
   185	Despite the generality of this result, we have nothing to say about
   186	optimal fillings of spheres of dimension 3 or higher.
   187	\end{comment}
   188	\end{abstract}
   189	
   190	\section{Overview}
   191	Let $\Delta = \Delta(\Omega)$ be the $|\Omega|\mns 1$-simplex
   192	with vertices $\Omega$,
   193	viewed as the abstract simplicial complex consisting of all subsets of
   194	$\Omega$.
   195	Let $C_n=C_n(\Omega)=C_n(\Delta(\Omega),\ZZ)$
   196	and $Z_n=Z_n(\Omega)=Z_n(\Delta(\Omega),\ZZ)$
   197	be its integral $n$-chains and $n$-cycles.
   198	A \emph{filling} of an $n$-cycle $X \in Z_n$ is any
   199	$n\pls 1$-chain $M \in C_{n+1}$ with $\del M = X$.
   200	(Here and throughout we assume $n\geq 1$.)
   201	Let $\Zvol(X)$ be the minimum $L_1$-norm of a filling of $X$.
   202	Call $M \in C_{n+1}$ \emph{taut} if it is an optimal filling
   203	of its boundary, i.e., if $|M|=\Zvol(\del M)$.
   204	
   205	For $X \in C_n$ let
   206	$\vertices(X)$ be the set of all the vertices of all the $n$-simplices
   207	to which $X$ assigns non-$0$ weight.
   208	For any taut filling $M$ of $X$ we have $\vertices(M) = \vertices(X)$.
   209	(Cf.\ Proposition \ref{nointernal} below.)
   210	This is why we write $C_n$ and $Z_n$ without reference to $\Omega$.
   211	
   212	Call an $n$-cycle $X+Y \in Z_n$ an \emph{almost disjoint union} if
   213	\[
   214	|\vertices(X) \cap \vertices(Y)| \leq n+1
   215	.
   216	\]
   217	This notion generalizes both disjoint union and connected sum
   218	along an $n$-simplex.
   219	Ellison \cite{ellison:gap}
   220	showed the $\Zvol$ adds under almost disjoint union:
   221	$\Zvol(X+Y) = \Zvol(X) + \Zvol(Y)$.
   222	This means that \emph{some} taut filling $M$ of $X$ splits into a sum
   223	$M=M_X+M_Y$ of taut fillings of $X,Y$.
   224	Here we amplify Ellison's Corollary 2 to show (Theorem \ref{th1})
   225	that when $n \geq 2$, \emph{any} taut filling of $X+Y$ splits.
   226	
   227	In place of integral chains we can use chains with
   228	coefficients in $\QQ$ or $\RR$.
   229	Evidently $\Rvol \leq \Qvol$.
   230	But computing $\Rvol$ is
   231	a linear programming problem with rational coefficients,
   232	so in fact $\Qvol = \Rvol$.
   233	Ellison used LP duality to prove that
   234	$\Qvol$ adds under almost
   235	disjoint union.
   236	Now for $n \geq 2$ we get the stronger result
   237	(Corollary \ref{cor1})
   238	that taut $\QQ$-fillings split:
   239	Multiplying a taut
   240	$\QQ$-filling $M$ of $X$ by a common denominator $q$
   241	yields a taut $\ZZ$-filling $qM$ of $qX$:
   242	Split the $\ZZ$-filling $qM$ and divide by $q$ to get a splitting
   243	of the $\QQ$-filling $M$.
   244	
   245	Now let $\sigma$ be a simplicial triangulation of $S^2$;
   246	let $X(\sigma) \in Z_2(\Delta(\vertices(\sigma)),\ZZ)$
   247	be either one of the 2-cycles
   248	that arises from $\sigma$ by orienting its $2$-simplices;
   249	and write $\Zvol(\sigma) = \Zvol(X(\sigma))$.
   250	Let $\tetvol(\sigma)$ be the minimum number of $3$-simplices
   251	required to extend $\sigma$ to a simplicial triangulation $\tau$ of $B^3$.
   252	We will show that
   253	\[
   254	\Zvol(\sigma) = \tetvol(\sigma)
   255	.
   256	\]
   257	Certainly $\Zvol \leq \tetvol$,
   258	because any extension $\tau$ induces a filling
   259	of $X(\sigma)$.
   260	Theorem \ref{th2} states that 
   261	any taut filling of $X(\sigma)$ arises in this way.
   262	The proof of Theorem \ref{th2} relies on Theorem \ref{th1}.
   263	\begin{comment}
   264	We use induction, with base case $\sigma$ a tetrahedron.
   265	In any taut filling $M$ of $X(\sigma)$,
   266	thought of as a multiset of oriented $3$-simplices,
   267	some $t \in M$ meets $\sigma$ in at least two faces.
   268	Ignoring the base case,
   269	$M-t$ is a taut filling of
